{"agent":"bg79","data":{"by":"grok-4.7","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":1,"detail":"The rewritten queued-claim refusal drops the holder and queue place the old text named (`queued behind {ahead.holder} ({ahead.item})`). Given Z waiting on `a.py` and W waiting on `b.py`, the caller gets `{item}: those files (…) are queued behind earlier waiters ([lease].wait_queue)`, a literal field path and no waiter per glob. `queued_behind` is only `aheads[0].item`, so the second waiter is gone from the structured field as well. That fails the stated contract that a refusal list every conflicting glob with its holder or queue place.","digest":"46a1a88ffa0aa1d7","family":"xai","location":"ddflow/api/lifecycle/claim_guard_queued.py:24","reviewer":"grok-4.7","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/api/lifecycle/claim_guard_queued.py:24"},{"chunk":1,"detail":"The reservation path has the same regression. Previously the reason was `{named} is queued behind {ahead.holder} ({ahead.item})`. Now it is `those files ({queued_names}) are queued behind earlier waiters ([lease].wait_queue)`, and `Blocked` is built with only `[aheads[0].item]`. An update blocked by two earlier waiters on two globs therefore names neither waiter and records only the first blocking item.","digest":"29ed57efd6b395ee","family":"xai","location":"ddflow/api/lifecycle/reservations.py:158","reviewer":"grok-4.7","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/api/lifecycle/reservations.py:158"},{"chunk":2,"detail":"The rewritten `update --globs` refusal still does not name holders or queue places. `queued_ahead` may collect every overlapping waiter, but the text thrown to the caller hard-codes `queued behind earlier waiters ([lease].wait_queue)`. Given an update of `a.py` and `b.py` while Z is queued on `a.py` and W on `b.py`, the caller sees `those globs (…) are queued behind earlier waiters ([lease].wait_queue)`, with a literal field path instead of each glob’s waiter — the same one-conflict/vague refusal B-glob-refusal-names-o